<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorsland Trek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thirstland Trek</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dorsland Trek ('Thirstland Trek') was a series of migrations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> families from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> across the Kalahari region between 1874 and 1881, seeking land and freedom from British influence. Many people and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> died of thirst and disease on the way through present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. The survivors settled at Humpata in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and some later founded the short-lived Republic of Upingtonia near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grootfontein">Grootfontein</a> in 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorsland-trek.jpg" alt="Dorsland Trek" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by Underlying lk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Route_of_the_Dorsland_Trekkers.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The trekkers came from a divided, poor and isolated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> community in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>. Religious grievances weighed most: opposition to the progressive policy of President Thomas Burgers, above all his new education laws, disputes within the Gereformeerde Kerk (Reformed Church) and the search for a New Jerusalem, so that most trekkers belonged to that church. With them went an estimated 130 to 200 labourers known as the mak volk, descendants of the indigenous people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, most taken as children on the battlefield and raised by the trekkers, who are often left out of accounts of the trek.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A first party of 10 families left in May 1875 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ngami">Lake Ngami</a> in September; Herero and Nama-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> captains, turned against them by the Cape envoy William Coates Palgrave, refused them land. A second, much larger party camped along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, where malaria began to kill, and on 7 May 1877 left Letloche as one disorderly column. At watering places such as Tlhabala and Lethlakane cattle stampeded and died, and people rationed water by the spoon or drank the blood of animals; none died of thirst, but malaria went on killing. A third party left in April 1877, the month <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>.</p><p>Along the Okavango, malaria and tsetse fly killed many people and most of the oxen; of one group of 65 under Gert du Preez, all the men died. The parties reunited at Leeupan in July 1878 and from May 1879 stayed for 18 months in Kaokoland. After agreements with Sebastião Nunes da Matta, governor of Moçâmedes, signed on 18 September and 28 December 1880, the Portuguese chose Humpata for their settlement on 4 January 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 700 people left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> between 1874 and 1877; roughly 230 died and about as many turned back, and although some 130 babies were born on the way, only 370 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. Portugal hoped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> would help it hold the interior against indigenous peoples and the British. By 1 July 1883 Humpata held 325 Boers and 43 mak volk. From August 1928 to February 1929 the South African government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a> repatriated 1,922 Boers, most to South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> of 1958 at Humpata, where the leader Gert Alberts is buried, and a memorial of 2003 at Cassinga commemorate the trek.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dorsland Trek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Boer <b>Dorsland</b> trekkers who had crossed the Kalahari settled around Grootfontein, on land bought in 1885 from the Ndonga king Kambonde by the trader William Worthington Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-upingtonia">Republic of Upingtonia</a></li></ul></section>
